# WHAT MAY NOT BE COMMITTED FROM HERE, and the reason is one file.
#
# config.env states a whole installation, and several of its values are credentials: the
# elevation password of the machine, repository write tokens, a repository read token, a DNS
# token, a storage password and a registry token. A token that reaches a remote must be
# rotated, and the mistake that puts it there is `git add .` made once.
#
# THIS FILE IS WHY THE LAUNCHERS ACCEPT A CONFIG STANDING HERE AT ALL. Every one of them
# refuses a config inside a git working tree that does NOT ignore it, and asks git itself rather
# than guessing: what is named here can no longer be swept up, and committing it takes a
# deliberate `git add -f`, which is somebody choosing rather than somebody slipping.
#
# config.example.env is NOT ignored and must not be: it carries no value, it is generated
# from what the five programs declare, and it is the thing an operator copies.
config.env

# AND ONE PER MACHINE, because a second machine is a second file. `config.apps3.env`
# beside `config.env` is the obvious way to keep two installations apart, and until this
# line existed git would have offered to commit it — a file of ten credentials, named
# after the machine it opens.
config.*.env

# The example is NOT ignored and must not be: it carries no value, it is generated from
# what the five programs declare, and it is the thing an operator copies. It is named
# again here because the line above would otherwise sweep it up.
!config.example.env

# Where install-machine.sh and install-machine.ps1 put the session transcript and the
# machine's own run records. Those carry no credential — the engine redacts a secret
# answer before it reaches a record — but they are one machine's history and belong to
# nobody else.
install-transcripts/
